ple usage\ncheck_unique_constraint(City, ['name', 'population'])\n```\n\nThis function will raise a `ValueError` if any of the specified fields do not exist in the model.\n\nIf you need to apply this check during migrations, you can create a custom migration operation. Here is an example:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db import migrations\n\nclass Migration(migrations.Migration):\n\n    dependencies = [\n        ('your_app_name', 'previous_migration_number'),\n    ]\n\n    operations = [\n        migrations.RunPython(\n            code=check_unique_constraint,\n            reverse_code=migrations.RunPython.noop,\n            args=('City', ['name', 'population']),\n        ),\n    ]\n```\n\nThis migration operation will run the `check_unique_constraint` function during the migration process.\n\nIf you need to modify binary files, you would typically use a library that can handle these formats, such as `shapefile` for `.shp`, `.shx`, and `.dbf` files. However, this is beyond the scope of standard Django operations and would require additional libraries and custom code.","round":1,"slot":0},"producer":{"instance":"01KZSCAQMQNRP84DTCYEKT18GN","kind":"drafter","parent":"01KZSCAQMKYYFY30ZGVTZ8BY8N"},"schema":"Candidate@1","seq":14,"t":1786484605.226722}
